My first ascension!  I've been wanting this for fifteen years.
Ascending feels great.  Those of you still working on your first
ascension, keep on hacking--it's totally worth it.

I ascended a monk wearing body armor, which is kind of unusual.  I
didn't ascend that way on purpose, though.  Read on for the stupid
details....


Contents:
    1.  Lessons learned (spoilers)
    2.  Story
    3.  Credits
    4.  Ascension stats


1.  Lessons learned (spoilers)

  * Individually name your cancellation wands something obvious like
    "NO BAGGIE" to remind you not to destroy your bag of holding by
    putting them inside it.

  * If you have bought protection from a priest, the protection you
    have bought will disappear if the priest dies.

  * "Enchant armor" scrolls can destroy your armor through
    over-enchantment.  Before reading a scroll of enchant armor,
    double-check what you are wearing to be sure the wrong thing
    doesn't get enchanted beyond what it can handle.


2.  Story

"spring," the neutral human monk, emerged from the monkery as a
fresh-faced Candidate and immediately descended into the bowels of the
earth--and subsequently into the bowels of several purple worms.  He
emerged almost 60,000 turns later as an immortal demigod to enjoy
fame, fortune, and a new career as a motivational speaker.

My favorite opening approach, which is what I used for "spring," is to
first descend into the Gnomish Mines to the level of Minetown, dork
around there for a while, go back up and solve Sokoban, and only then
descend to the bottom of the Mines.

This worked like a champ.  I got Mjollnir from the Minetown altar
(and--ooh!--Trollbane), a bag of holding from Sokoban, and a luckstone
from the Catacombs at the bottom of the Mines.  Good enough.  Let's
roll.

I know what you're thinking.  "A monk with a weapon?  It's so crazy it
just might work!"  Well, that's not what I was thinking at all.  This
was my first time playing a monk, and I wasn't confident that I could
last the whole game with just martial arts.  So I split the difference
(badly) by #enhancing my martial arts skills up to their maximum
before chickening out and finally using a weapon, thus wasting
beaucoup enhancement slots.

Down, down, down, be-dooby down into the dungeon I went, my dog
trotting behind me.  At some point, my dog went wandering off and came
back as a hill giant.  I never did find out where that polymorph trap
was, but at the time I was happy to have some firepower on my side.

Since monks have a major to-hit penalty attached to wearing body
armor, getting a decent armor class can be a problem.  So first
priority for me was to collect enough dinero to buy some quality
protection from a priest.  The money was coming OK.  Problem was,
there weren't any priests except the one in Minetown.  It was a super
drag having to climb up and down to drop my money off at Protection
Central.  Luckily, I had a magic whistle to bring my hill giant
along.

I began to see the downside of having a hill giant as a pet when,
while I was locked in the Minetown temple on a polypiling jag, my hill
giant playfully busted down the door so he could come in and see what
I was up to.  Me:  "Hello?  Little privacy here?"

I finally pay the priest enough to get my naked armor class down to 3.
Good enough for now.  Let's go to the hard levels.

Medusa gave me a scare.  I didn't have a way of crossing water at the
time, so I dug down through the floor, fought my way to the upstairs,
and went up, forgetting that that would put me right in Medusa's lap
without a blindfold on.  What should have been Yet Another Stupid
Death for me became Yet Another Stupid Victory due to the fact that,
at the last moment, she caught sight of herself in my polished silver
shield and didn't like what she saw.  Whew!

The castle was good to me.  A ring of conflict made things a lot
easier.  The guaranteed wand of wishing gave me many goodies.  Next
time, I should name my wand of wishing "Santa."

In retrospect, the best wishes I made were for blessed fireproof +2
speed boots and, on a whim, the blessed fireproof Staff of
Aesculapius.  Either of those guys is pretty sweet, but with both, I
probably should have gotten "BAD MAN" tattooed across my forehead to
give all the monsters fair warning before they died.  It's double
damage done damn quickly, plus level drain on a lot of creatures, plus
the Staff protects my own levels from being drained in the bargain.
And, needless to say, hungerless regeneration is the gift that keeps
on giving.  The Staff blasted me every time I picked it up, but with
its regeneration in effect, my hit points bounced back in a turn or
two--no worries there.

Ok, now I've got enough moolah to pay the priest for the remainder of
my protection.  Me and my little hill giant friend go up, uppity, up
the stairs, and down once more to Minetown.  Before paying the priest,
I nip over to the shops to make sure there's nothing I want to buy.  I
come back a few turns later, and my hill giant is sitting at the
temple altar on top of a pile of 30,000 zorkmids formerly owned by the
priest.  My giant is wiping his blood-covered hands with the priest's
empty robe, and he's got this self-satisfied grin plastered on his
face like a billboard for stupidity.

Did you know that if your protection priest dies, your protection goes
away?

After I stopped screaming, I smacked my giant across the nose with a
rolled-up newspaper, scooped the zorkmids into my bag of holding, and
went looking for another priest to donate to.  As it turned out, there
were no more aligned priests in the whole game.  That's right:  only
one non-Moloch priest in the whole freaking game, and my pet kills
him.  I have no idea how I ascended.

At some point right about here, I was rearranging stuff in my
luggage and accidentally put a wand of cancellation in my bag of
holding.  This blew up my bag of holding along with most of my stuff
in it, including about twelve spellbooks.  This time I had to smack my
own nose with the rolled-up newspaper.  It was a bad night.

After the hard levels, I went on the Quest.  Master Kaen (or "Special
K" as I like call him) was pretty hard, but not as hard as I feared.
He kicked like a mule, but I kicked harder.  I led him upstairs from
his little lava-hole, and we worked things out like men.

Gehennom wasn't that interesting, frankly.  I was tentative at first,
but as I came to realize that nothing was hitting me very hard except
Minotaurs, which I could handle, I started moving faster and faster.
Hardest was Asmodeus, but only because I didn't know I needed to lead
teleporting demons like him downstairs first, then sit on the upstairs
to keep them from teleporting or climbing out of my deadly grasp.

The Eyes of the Overworld from the monk quest came in handy for
mapping out the Gehennom mazes.  I would hate to have done without
them.

I fell down a trap door, then went up into Vlad's tower by accident
while trying to get back up to the previous level.  Vlad and his lil'
vampire buddies were easy, since no one could drain my levels.
(Bumper sticker I want:  "I heart my Staff.")

In the basement, I found the vibrating square that serves as subwoofer
for the whole dungeon, engraved it with "Elbereth," and dropped off my
candelabrum, candles, and bell.

That found, we're off to see the Wizard.  I found a wand of death
somewhere along the roadside, and I got a chance to put it to good use
when I finally peeled the lid back from Rodney's can of sardines.  One
hit did the trick.  Rodney died; nobody cried; I got the Book of the
Dead.  Everyone's happy except Rodney, and who cares about some freak
who lives in a windowless, doorless room surrounded by a moat, filled
with squeaky boards, with nobody but a vampire lord and a black dragon
to talk to?  I hope he had cable at least, for his sake.

Candle, Bell, and Book.  Kaboom!  Down we go.

I fought my way through a cloud of demons, dragons, disenchanters,
priestesses, and other Moloch miscellanea to collect the biggest and
blingiest amulet of them all: the Amulet of Yendor.  I got it
appraised, promptly labelled it "Au-then-tique" to avoid terrible
future misunderstandings, then headed upwards towards that big flea
market in the sky.

Someone should really look into the source of that mysterious force
that kept tossing me downstairs every time I tried to go up in
Gehennom.  Very annoying.  In fact, I think I'll go take care of it
right now.  With the Amulet returned, I seem to have some time on my
hands.  My new demigod powers will make short work of it, no doubt.

Around level eight, I stopped at an altar to make some holy water out
of some useless potions I had diluted.  "These malignant auras are
getting me down; best to have some holy water in reserve.  Ta da!
Lots of holy water now.  What shall I do with it?  I have some
'enchant armor' scrolls left over, and they won't do me any good in
heaven; let's dip them in holy water and enchant up some of my
weirder boots."

One mistake later, and I read a blessed "enchant armor" scroll while
wearing all my fully charged armor.  My cloak of magic resistance
crumbled into dust.

This, in case you couldn't figure that out, was super bad.  I was not
going to survive the Planes without magic resistance of ###.  I didn't
have another cloak of magic resistance lying around anywhere that I
could remember.  Resurrected rappin' Rodney was on my tail about some
amulet that he never even owned to begin with, so I didn't have
forever to look for one, either.  Had I, though a vicious act of
stupidity, just lost the game?

A spare robe covered my nakedness, but nothing could cover my shame.
At a loss for what to do next, I headed down to Fort Ludios to see
whether there was anything I could use in the stuff left over from
my battle there.

While kicking morosely through the soldier skulls, blood-spattered
daggers, and huge piles of C-rations at Ludios, I happened upon a
dwarvish mithril coat.  Dwarvish mithril coat?

"You're a monk," I thought, "Body armor's no good for you.  You'll
never hit anything wearing that stuff.  It'll chafe.  It probably
smells like sweaty dwarves.  And yet...."

Guess what?  It chafed like hell.  Every single time I took a swing at
something, I got the message, "Your armor is rather cumbersome...
--More--"  Doing battle in that dorky suit nearly wore out my
spacebar.

But I had magic resistance of ### on my back, my (very carefully
enchanted) new armor class was decreased by about eight points, and,
best of all, I could still _hit_ stuff while wearing it!  I think I
only missed my target once for the rest of the game.  Body armor was
very, very good to me.  Accidentally destroying my cloak of magic
resistance turned out to be an incredible stroke of luck for me.

The Plane of Earth was trivial.

The Plane of Air started hairy and got hairier.  With the help of my
levitation boots and ring of conflict, I made it over to right side of
the screen after an eternity or two.  By then I had a huge, brawling
posse of dragons and djinns and elementals and vortices behind me, all
shooting rays and reading create monster scrolls and engulfing one
another.  Things were seriously out of control.  I feared an
exponential explosion in the monster population, where, by reading
"create monster" scrolls, every monster on the plane would create on
average 1.5 monsters before it died, thus filling the screen in a
matter of minutes and preventing me from doing anything except holding
down the spacebar to scroll through all the monster battle messages.

At long last, I was able to kill a cockatrice and use its corpse to
get ahead of the monster-spawning curve.  A few statues later, I found
the portal and was on my way.

Scariest moment of the game:  I got engulfed by a fire vortex on the
Plane of Fire, and when it was done with me, it tossed me into lava.
I was sinking fast into the lava with no hope of escape.  An
incredible piece of luck saved me, though.  Another vortex came by and
scooped me out of the lava before I could sink out of sight.  While
inside the second vortex, I had time to put on my levitation boots,
thus preventing that problem for the rest of the level.

The Plane of Water was dull.  I killed water elementals to pass the
time and waited for the portal to show up.  Now I know why they don't
make kraken sushi--that stuff is nasty.

The Astral Plane:  almost home.  On arrival, I spent a little time
rearranging my inventory and throwing away old wands.  By the time I
went to pick a door, it turned out that Pestilence had already opened
the middle one for me and was waiting in the hall.  Whoops.  I guess
I'm visiting the middle one first.

It says a lot about the hostile environment of the Astral Plane that
being surrounded by an attacking cloud of giant, stinging insects is
delighfully refreshing compared to everything else.  It's like some
kind of nouveau spa treatment.

Some odd folks were waiting for me in the middle antechamber.  "Who
the heck is 'Pat the Wizard'?" I said.  "Whoever he is, he's got one
hell of an armor class, and his hit points rule."  Lucky for me, Pat
was busy battling pretty much everybody else at this point.  I do so
love my ring of conflict.

Middle altar was lawful, but I needed neutral.  Time to move on.

The left altar had Death guarding it.  I blasted him a few times with
some samples from my wand of lightning collection.  An angel finished
him off--ring of conflict to the rescue again--and I drove in towards
the basket.

The hardest adjustment for me in the late game was learning to focus
on my goal rather than on killing everything I could see.  This was
especially true for the Astral Plane--you have to keep moving towards
the altar.  If you stop to kill stuff, sooner or later somebody will
show up you don't want to meet.  So I had to retrain myself to ignore
the monsters as much as possible and move forward whenever there was
space to move.

Yes!  Neutral altar!  I slammed down the Amulet, offered it to my god,
and got my nickel deposit back.  Case closed.


